{"answer":"Let S_n=1+3+5+...+(2n-1). We prove S_n=n^2 by induction. For n=1, S_1=1=1^2. Suppose S_k=k^2 for some k>=1. Then S_{k+1}=S_k+(2(k+1)-1)=k^2+2k+1=(k+1)^2. Hence, by induction, S_n=n^2 for every positive integer n.","approaches":[{"description":"Induction on the number of odd summands, using that the next summand is 2k+1.","id":"induction-proof","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It supplies a base case and proves the implication from k to k+1."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks for the identity for every positive integer n.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"target-identity","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The target is to show that S_n=1+3+5+...+(2n-1) equals n^2 for every positive integer n.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Computed in step-base.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"base-case","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"S_1=1^2.","step_ids":["step-base"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Induction hypothesis introduced for the k-to-k+1 subproof.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-inductive"],"id":"induction-hypothesis","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["step-inductive"],"statement":"For an arbitrary positive integer k, assume S_k=k^2.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-inductive under the induction hypothesis, which is discharged there.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["induction-hypothesis"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"inductive-step","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every positive integer k, S_k=k^2 implies S_{k+1}=(k+1)^2.","step_ids":["step-inductive"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Principle of mathematical induction, applied in step-induction-application using the base case and inductive implication.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["base-case","inductive-step"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"conclusion","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every positive integer n, S_n=n^2.","step_ids":["step-base","step-inductive","step-induction-application"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-base","justification":"S_1 consists of its sole summand 1, and 1=1^2.","statement":"Establish S_1=1^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-inductive","justification":"Under the scoped hypothesis S_k=k^2, append the next odd summand: S_{k+1}=S_k+(2(k+1)-1)=k^2+2k+1=(k+1)^2. This discharges the induction-hypothesis claim and proves the conditional implication for arbitrary k.","statement":"For arbitrary k>=1, prove S_k=k^2 implies S_{k+1}=(k+1)^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-base","step-inductive"],"id":"step-induction-application","justification":"Apply the principle of mathematical induction to the base case and the proved implication.","statement":"Conclude S_n=n^2 for every positive integer n."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
